- touch creates empty files if they don't exist.
We edited the files using Nano:
nano compose.yaml
nano package.json
nano app.js


In Nano, we saved with Ctrl + O, pressed Enter, and exited with Ctrl + X.
4. Our compose.yaml file
This is our final configuration, including the corrected host port 3002.
services:
  redis:
    image: redis:7-alpine
    ports:
      - "6379:6379"

  app:
    image: node:24-alpine
    working_dir: /app
    volumes:
      - .:/app
    command: sh -c "npm install && node app.js"
    ports:
      - "3002:3000"
    depends_on:
      - redis


What each section means
Configuration	Purpose
redis	Defines our Redis service
image: redis:7-alpine	Uses the Redis image
app	Defines our Node.js service
image: node:24-alpine	Uses Node.js on Alpine Linux
working_dir: /app	Sets the working directory inside the container
volumes: - .:/app	Mounts the project directory into /app
command	Installs dependencies and starts the app
depends_on	Starts Redis before the app service
Notice that depends_on controls startup order; it doesn't by itself guarantee Redis is ready to accept connections. Our app also calls redisClient.connect() before starting its web server.
Understanding the port mapping
Our final mapping is:
ports:
  - "3002:3000"

Your browser / Ubuntu host
Port 3002
Node.js container
Port 3000

The first number is the host port; the second is the container port. This is why we open http://localhost:3002, even though Node.js listens on port 3000 inside the container.
