8. Troubleshooting: The old web page kept appearing
This was the main problem we solved together.
We expected the new page:
<h1>Hello from KELY!</h1>
<p>Visits: 1</p>

Instead, requests from Ubuntu returned the old page:
<title>KELY's Practice</title>
<h1>Hello from KELY's Practice</h1>

The old page had a light-blue background. We did not want to delete our previous project just to make this one work.
Problem 1: The host returned the old page
We ran:
curl http://localhost:3000

It returned the old HTML.
We also tested:
curl http://127.0.0.1:3000

That returned the same old HTML.
What we learned: changing from localhost to 127.0.0.1 did not solve the issue. Both addresses reached the same old response.
Problem 2: We checked the running app's logs
docker compose logs app

The logs showed that the app had started successfully.
We then inspected the file inside the running container:
docker compose exec app cat /app/app.js

This confirmed that the container had the new app.js code.
Problem 3: We tested from inside the app container
We opened a shell inside the container:
docker compose exec app sh


Our prompt changed to:
/app #

Inside that shell, we ran:
cat app.js

Again, the new code was present.
We initially tried:
curl http://localhost:3000

But the container responded:
sh: curl: not found


Why? The node:24-alpine image is minimal and doesn't include curl by default.
Rather than installing another utility unnecessarily, we used Node.js's built-in fetch:
node -e "fetch('http://localhost:3000').then(r => r.text()).then(console.log).catch(console.error)"


The result was:
<h1>Hello from KELY!</h1><p>Visits: 1</p>

This was an important discovery: the new application was working inside its container, and Redis was responding to the visit-counter request.
We exited the container shell with:
exit

That returned us to the Ubuntu host terminal.
Problem 4: We checked the Docker context
We ran:
docker context show

Output:
default

Then:
docker context ls

We found two available contexts:
NAME            DESCRIPTION                               DOCKER ENDPOINT
default *       Current DOCKER_HOST based configuration   unix:///var/run/docker.sock
desktop-linux   Docker Desktop                            npipe:////./pipe/dockerDesktopLinuxEngine

We attempted to inspect running containers in the second context:
docker --context desktop-linux ps

It failed:
Failed to initialize: protocol not available

We did not change contexts. We continued troubleshooting with the working default context.
Problem 5: We inspected the port mapping
We ran:
docker inspect docker-compose-practice-app-1 --format '{{json .NetworkSettings.Ports}}'

Output:
{"3000/tcp":[{"HostIp":"0.0.0.0","HostPort":"3000"},{"HostIp":"::","HostPort":"3000"}]}

This confirmed that the running app container still had host port 3000 mapped to container port 3000.
We also checked the container's IP address:
docker inspect docker-compose-practice-app-1 --format '{{range .NetworkSettings.Networks}}{{.IPAddress}}{{end}}'

Output:
172.18.0.3

We tried connecting to it directly:
curl http://172.18.0.3:3000

The request appeared to hang, so we used a timeout and verbose output:
curl -v --max-time 5 http://172.18.0.3:3000

The output showed:
* Trying 172.18.0.3:3000...
* Connection timed out after 5002 milliseconds

This told us that direct access to the container IP from the Ubuntu host was timing out. It did not mean the application itself was broken, since the internal Node.js fetch had already succeeded.
Problem 6: We checked the port listener
We ran:
sudo ss -ltnp '( sport = :3000 )'

The output showed:
LISTEN ... 0.0.0.0:3000 ... users:(("docker-proxy",pid=959,fd=7))
LISTEN ... [::]:3000    ... users:(("docker-proxy",pid=965,fd=7))

This confirmed that Docker's proxy was listening on port 3000 in Ubuntu.
At this point, we had evidence that the new app worked inside the container, but the host request still returned old content.

9. The fix: Change the host port and recreate the app
Instead of stopping or deleting older containers, we decided to use a different host port.

Step 1: Edit compose.yaml
We opened the file:
nano compose.yaml

We changed the app port mapping from:
ports:
  - "3000:3000"

to:
ports:
  - "3002:3000"

We left the Redis mapping unchanged:
ports:
  - "6379:6379"

We saved the file and exited Nano.
Step 2: Test the new port
We first ran:
curl http://localhost:3002

It failed:
curl: (7) Failed to connect to localhost port 3002

Why? Because the saved file had changed, but the running container still had the old port mapping.
We verified the saved file:
cat compose.yaml

It correctly showed:
ports:
  - "3002:3000"

However, docker compose ps -a still showed the old 3000:3000 mapping.

This was the key distinction: the saved configuration and the running container configuration did not match.
Step 3: Recreate only the app service
We ran:
docker compose up -d --force-recreate app

Docker reported:
✔ Container docker-compose-practice-redis-1 Running
✔ Container docker-compose-practice-app-1 Started


Redis remained running. Docker recreated the app container with the updated configuration.
Step 4: Verify the new port mapping
We ran:
docker compose ps -a

The output now showed:
docker-compose-practice-app-1   ...   Up   0.0.0.0:3002->3000/tcp
docker-compose-practice-redis-1 ...   Up   0.0.0.0:6379->6379/tcp


Step 5: Test the application again
We ran:

curl http://localhost:3002

10. Why recreating the container was the right choice
You suggested that we might need to restart something. Your instinct to refresh the service was good!
The important distinction is:
Command	What it does
docker compose restart app	Restarts the existing app container
docker compose up -d app	Brings the app service toward the configuration in the Compose file
docker compose up -d --force-recreate app	Forces the app container to be recreated
A regular restart doesn't recreate the container with a new port mapping. Our saved file specified 3002:3000, while the running container still had 3000:3000. Recreating the app applied the new mapping.
We targeted only the app service, so we avoided unnecessary disruption to Redis.

11. Commands worth remembering Start the project docker compose up -d

Starts services in the background.

Check service status and ports
docker compose ps -a

Shows the Compose containers, status, and published ports.

Inspect application logs
docker compose logs app

Helps investigate startup failures and application errors.

Run a command inside a service
docker compose exec app cat /app/app.js

Runs cat inside the running app container.

Recreate one service
docker compose up -d --force-recreate app

Forces the app container to be recreated with the configured settings.

